## 6. Channel alignment
One avsync session (module load 4) delivered every burst with the words offset by one: left held
R[n-1], right L[n] (left 0, 0, 4240, 8192, ...; right 0, 2139, 4240, ...). It held for that whole
session, through 30 stream restarts (lr1) and 10 domain resets (lr2):
  channel alignment per burst: {'swapped': 14}
  channel alignment per burst: {'swapped': 62}
  channel alignment per burst: {'swapped': 40}
Every other session was aligned: 12 module reloads with a new avsync session each, 40 avsync
sessions in one load (avsync --off, on, 2.6 s capture), and all runs above but a5/lr1/lr2:
  reload 1: {'aligned': 3}; reload 2: {'aligned': 3}; reload 3: {'aligned': 3}; reload 4: {'aligned': 3}; reload 5: {'aligned': 3}; reload 6: {'aligned': 3}; reload 7: {'aligned': 3}; reload 8: {'aligned': 3}; reload 9: {'aligned': 3}; reload 10: {'aligned': 3}; reload 11: {'aligned': 3}; reload 12: {'aligned': 3}
  session 1: {'aligned': 3}; session 2: {'aligned': 3}; session 3: {'aligned': 2}; session 4: {'aligned': 3}; session 5: {'aligned': 2}; session 6: {'aligned': 2}; session 7: {'aligned': 3}; session 8: {'aligned': 2}; session 9: {'aligned': 2}; session 10: {'aligned': 2}; session 11: {'aligned': 2}; session 12: {'aligned': 3}; session 13: {'aligned': 3}; session 14: {'aligned': 2}; session 15: {'aligned': 3}; session 16: {'aligned': 3}; session 17: {'aligned': 2}; session 18: {'aligned': 3}; session 19: {'aligned': 2}; session 20: {'aligned': 2}; session 21: {'aligned': 2}; session 22: {'aligned': 2}; session 23: {'aligned': 2}; session 24: {'aligned': 2}; session 25: {'aligned': 3}; session 26: {'aligned': 2}; session 27: {'aligned': 3}; session 28: {'aligned': 2}; session 29: {'aligned': 2}; session 30: {'aligned': 2}; session 31: {'aligned': 3}; session 32: {'aligned': 2}; session 33: {'aligned': 2}; session 34: {'aligned': 2}; session 35: {'aligned': 2}; session 36: {'aligned': 2}; session 37: {'aligned': 3}; session 38: {'aligned': 2}; session 39: {'aligned': 2}; session 40: {'aligned': 2}
So 1 session of 53+ had the swap; what sets it is not known (the source, the IT6805 audio FIFO, or
the FPGA's word phase). The driver has no traced control for it. RD's untraced audio paths, an
audio logic reset on 0:b9 bits 7:6 or on 0:10 bit 7 (spec/hdmi-it6805.md 11.1), are skipped by the
driver; it now counts both (debugfs hdmi, "it6805: audio errors N events N").

per-burst check (lr.py): aligned = left [0, 2139, ...] with right [0, 4240, ...] from the same frame;
swapped = left [0, 0, 4240, 8192] with right [0, 2139, 4240]

## 7. Fresh audio start (source audio stopped >= 5 s, then avsync.sh), exp_audio_raw=1, 4 runs
The capture runs from 1 s before the source starts. AUDIO_SAMPLE_PERIOD turns valid at read 141,
138, 149, 144 (1.38-1.49 s after the source started); the first sample, the source's first burst,
follows 4-5 periods later and arrives complete (left 2139, 4240, ..., right 4240, 8192, ... from its
second sample). No period holds a sample while AUDIO_SAMPLE_PERIOD reads all ones (0 of 4 runs).
Started the moment the IT6805 audio state went ON, a capture saw AUDIO_SAMPLE_PERIOD all ones for
another 12-14 periods (silent +14, +12 with the policy): silence the hardware gives anyway. The
IT6805's audio event (0:10 bit 7 while STABLE, untraced) counter rose by 1-2 at each source audio
start or stop (3, 4, 4, 6, 7, 8, 9 over these runs); no event without such a transition was seen.

## 8. Ten minutes of audio and video (tests/live.sh avsync, log 20261125-052609)
600.0 s, 28800960 frames (48001.265 Hz against CLOCK_MONOTONIC, +26.4 ppm), no overrun, read
timestamps within 0.41 ms of a line (per-second medians), 600 bursts with the channels right,
35997 video frames without a sequence gap. A/V offset (burst onset - flash frame, frame-corrected)
-23.555 ms, std 0.054 ms, drift -0.0028 ms/min (-0.028 ms over the run). Without input the periods
ran at +5 ppm against the clock (run 1, 3 s); with the source they run at its rate and do not drift
against its video: with a live source the source's audio clock paces the periods, not the FPGA's
(spec/audio.md "Running", the question it leaves open). The absolute offset differs between avsync
sessions (-14.38 ms in a 20 s run of another session): the source's own audio latency.
